Internet of Things deployments increasingly replicate telemetry across
independent clouds for availability and vendor independence, and it must stay
encrypted once it leaves the device domain. Replication buys privacy only if
the providers cannot pool what they observe. Systems that split trust across
providers to hide search access patterns exist, but they operate on plaintext,
stop at retrieval, or need an honest majority and server-to-server
interaction. The unsolved step is composition: selecting the matching records
privately is worthless if aggregating over them hands the matched set back to
the providers. We present CR-SHE, in which retrieval and analytics are both
oblivious to a coalition of up to $t=n-1$ colluding providers, over data
encrypted at rest under one owner's key and with no communication between
providers. \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{We first show that under these constraints a homomorphic layer is
necessary: an additively shared store cannot answer such queries locally, and
any scheme that lets it do so yields single-server private information
retrieval. }\DIFaddend The core construction is oblivious homomorphic aggregation: each
provider folds its \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{distributed point function share into the encrypted record
column }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{share of the selection vector into encrypted columns }\DIFaddend and
returns one ciphertext, so the matched set is never materialised and its
cardinality never leaves the client. A precomputed-column fast path removes the
dependence on record count, \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{a two-round flexible path serves conjunctions and
ad-hoc weights by dealing fresh shares of any selection vector, }\DIFaddend and a
one-time-pad index mask \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{,
cancelled inside the ciphertext, removes posting-list-length }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{removes posting-list }\DIFaddend leakage at rest. We prove
correctness and adaptive leakage security against $t=n-1$ collusion \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{,
and
evaluate a prototype }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{and
evaluate }\DIFaddend on two real IoT corpora. On $405{,}184$ sensor records a registered
aggregate costs $39$\,ms per provider irrespective of record count\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, with a fixed $526$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{; carrying
the value and its MAC in the two rows of one BFV ciphertext returns four
aggregates in a fixed $132$}\DIFaddend \,kB response \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{and $617\times$ less workthan a
faithful homomorphic equality scan; a }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{instead of $2.1$\,MB. Against one
server holding the same encrypted columns, the trust split saves only
$1.8$--$2.9\times$ in server work, but over $3{,}000\times$ in uplink and
$27$--$108\times$ in client-side query cost, which is what a thin IoT client
pays for. A }\DIFaddend frequency
attack recovering every non-temporal metadata tag from an unmasked index
recovers none from the masked one.
